# Hook return shape

For complex custom hooks that expose multiple concerns, group returned values by purpose instead of a large flat object. Use these group names only when needed — never return empty groups:

- `data` — source state, computed values, derived data
- `handlers` — callbacks, event handlers, actions
- `statuses` — loading, error, success, and other operation states
- `refs` — React refs exposed to consumers

Keep simple hooks simple: a single value or a small cohesive API should use the most natural return shape without grouping.

When extending existing hooks or wrapping third-party hooks, preserve established API contracts. Do not add `useMemo` / `useCallback` only to stabilize grouped return objects — React Compiler handles memoization (see `react-compiler` rule).

```ts
// ❌ BAD — flat bag of unrelated concerns
return { trips, selectedTrip, handleSelectTrip, handleSave, isLoading, isSaving, error, searchInputRef }

// ✅ GOOD — grouped by purpose (omit unused groups)
return {
  data: { trips, selectedTrip },
  handlers: { handleSelectTrip, handleSave },
  statuses: { isLoading, isSaving, error },
  refs: { searchInputRef },
}

// ✅ GOOD — simple hook stays ungrouped
return value
```
